course: dictionary
lesson: 7821
title: Quill
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. The word quill is a good example. «Before metal nibs and ballpoint pens, people wrote with quills made from goose feathers.» Here a quill is a pen, because the sentence is about writing.
explain: Now read this: «A North American porcupine has about 30,000 quills, stiff hairs with tiny barbs that work their way into skin.» A pen would not fit here. The clues are porcupine and skin, so quills must be the sharp spines on the porcupine's body.
explain: A quill can also be a large, stiff feather from a bird's wing or tail. To choose the right meaning, put each one into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found it.
fact: A quill is a pen made from a large bird feather, with the end sharpened and dipped in ink.
fact: A quill can also mean a large, stiff feather from a bird's wing or tail.
fact: The sharp spines on a porcupine or hedgehog are also called quills.
fact: «Porcupines cannot shoot their quills; the quills come off easily when touched and stick into the attacker.»
fact: «A porcupine rattles its quills and stamps its feet as a warning first.»
exercise: In «Before metal nibs, people wrote with quills made from goose feathers», what is a quill? | A bird's song | A type of ink | *A pen made from a feather | A spiky porcupine
exercise: «A porcupine rattles its quills and stamps its feet as a warning.» What are the quills here? | Writing pens | Pieces of paper | Long feathers for flying | *Sharp spines
exercise: Which sentence uses quill to mean a pen? | *«She dipped her quill in the ink and began to write.» | «The bird dropped a long, grey quill on the lawn.» | «The hedgehog curled up and raised its quills.»
test: «Writers and wizards in stories often write with quills.» What does quill mean here? | *A pen made from a bird feather | A type of paper | A large bird feather | A dish for holding ink
test: Why does a porcupine rattle its quills? | To shake off dust | To dry them in the sun | To sing | *To warn an attacker
exam: In «A North American porcupine has about 30,000 quills», quills means | Feathers from wings | Pens | Pieces of paper | *Sharp spines
